## Why this approach works
This architecture ensures that all allocated memory remains "reachable" by the program, preventing the operating system from reclaiming the memory through garbage collection or optimization. By chaining the pointers, we force the heap to grow until the kernel refuses further requests, successfully exhausting the system's available resources.

## Explanation
The `fork()` function is a special command in C that tells your computer to create an exact copy of the current program. Imagine you are a manager (the "parent process") and you need to finish a big project; you use `fork()` to hire an assistant (the "child process") who is an identical clone of you. Both you and your assistant start working from the exact same line of code at the same time, but you can tell who is who by looking at the result the `fork()` function gives back to you.

## Use Cases
In programming, `fork()` is used to achieve **multitasking**. By creating a child process, your program can perform two different tasks at the same time—for example, one part of the program could handle user input while the other part processes data in the background. This makes programs much more efficient and responsive.

